  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "q_triads-ch5-checkpoint.what-moves-least",
      "kind": "choice",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "text": "You are comping on one string set and the next chord is coming. What does voice leading tell you to do first?"
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Look for a note both chords contain and leave that finger exactly where it is — then move everything else the shortest distance it can go."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Move all three fingers the same distance, so the shape arrives intact."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Go to the next chord's root position, wherever it sits on the neck."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Take the inversion that keeps the same note in the bass as the chord before it."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "answerId": "a",
      "explanation": [
        {
          "text": "It is one idea rather than two rules: the smallest possible move is no move at all, so a shared note — a common tone — gets held, and only then does the rest of the hand travel, as little as it can. Moving all three fingers the same distance is what happens when there is "
        },
        {
          "text": "nothing",
          "marks": ["italic"]
        },
        {
          "text": " to hold, which is the "
        },
        {
          "text": "F",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " to "
        },
        {
          "text": "G",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " change in chapter 2's progression, not the general rule. Heading for the next chord's root position wherever it sits is the barre-chord habit this whole chapter is built against."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "q_triads-ch5-checkpoint.nothing-to-hold",
      "kind": "choice",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "text": "Going from "
        },
        {
          "text": "C",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " second inversion, "
        },
        {
          "text": "x x x 0 1 0",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", to "
        },
        {
          "text": "G",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " first inversion, "
        },
        {
          "text": "x x x 4 3 3",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", all three strings move: "
        },
        {
          "text": "+4 +2 +3",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". Both chords contain a "
        },
        {
          "text": "G",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". Why can't that note be held?"
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "In the "
            },
            {
              "text": "C",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " grip the shared note is "
            },
            {
              "text": "G3",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " on string 3; in the "
            },
            {
              "text": "G",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " grip it is "
            },
            {
              "text": "G4",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " on string 1. A common tone can only be held if it is the same pitch on the same string."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "They don't really share a note — the "
            },
            {
              "text": "G",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " in "
            },
            {
              "text": "C",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " major is a fifth and the "
            },
            {
              "text": "G",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " in "
            },
            {
              "text": "G",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " major is a root."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "There is a nearer copy of "
            },
            {
              "text": "G",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " on strings 1-2-3 that would have kept it; "
            },
            {
              "text": "x x x 4 3 3",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " is the wrong choice."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Common tones can only be held between chords a fourth or a fifth apart."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "answerId": "a",
      "explanation": [
        {
          "text": "The rule is about pitches in voices, not about note names. "
        },
        {
          "text": "G",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " major's three grips on this set sit at frets 3–4, 7–8 and 10–12, and none of them contains "
        },
        {
          "text": "G3",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " — so there was no nearer copy that would have kept it. Calling them different notes because one is a fifth and the other a root confuses the degree a note plays in a chord with the note itself; the pitch class is the same either way. When there is genuinely nothing to hold, you slide, and that is the rule reporting honestly rather than failing."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "q_triads-ch5-checkpoint.why-v-is-uppercase",
      "kind": "choice",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "text": "The seven triads of C major are written "
        },
        {
          "text": "I ii iii IV V vi vii°",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". Why is "
        },
        {
          "text": "V",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " uppercase?"
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Because the chord built on "
            },
            {
              "text": "G",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " is major. Case carries quality and nothing else."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Because it is the most important chord in the key after "
            },
            {
              "text": "I",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Because it is the chord that leads back to "
            },
            {
              "text": "I",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Because odd-numbered scale degrees are written uppercase."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "answerId": "a",
      "explanation": [
        {
          "text": "Case tracks what the chord sounds like, never what job it does: "
        },
        {
          "text": "I",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", "
        },
        {
          "text": "IV",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and "
        },
        {
          "text": "V",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " are uppercase because those three triads come out major; "
        },
        {
          "text": "ii",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", "
        },
        {
          "text": "iii",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and "
        },
        {
          "text": "vi",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " are lowercase because those come out minor. The odd-degrees-are-uppercase idea falls apart on "
        },
        {
          "text": "iii",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " — an odd degree, written lowercase, because the chord on the third degree of a major scale is minor."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "q_triads-ch5-checkpoint.which-inversion-fits",
      "kind": "choice",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "text": "Harmonised on strings 1-2-3 in second inversion, the whole C major scale fits between the nut and fret 13. Try the same run in root position and it runs off the end of the neck. Why?"
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Root position starts at fret 5 and climbs an octave, so the chord on the seventh degree would need fret 16 — past the end of a 15-fret neck. Second inversion starts at the nut because its bass is the fifth, and the fifth of "
            },
            {
              "text": "C",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " is the open third string."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Root-position grips are wider, so the run gives out on hand span before it gives out on neck."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "There is no root-position grip for "
            },
            {
              "text": "Bdim",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " on strings 1-2-3 at all."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Root position puts the third on top, and the high "
            },
            {
              "text": "e",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " string can't reach it."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "answerId": "a",
      "explanation": [
        {
          "text": "Both runs climb an octave; they just start in different places. Second inversion gets to open at fret 0 because the note it puts underneath is "
        },
        {
          "text": "G",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", which is what string 3 is already tuned to. It is not that "
        },
        {
          "text": "Bdim",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " root position has no grip on this set — it does exist, at "
        },
        {
          "text": "x x x 4 3 1",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", perfectly playable; it is only the copy an octave up, at fret 16, that doesn't exist. And the high e string is never asked for the third: in root position the degrees run "
        },
        {
          "text": "1 3 5",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", so it is the fifth on top, not the third."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
