        {
          "text": " → "
        },
        {
          "text": "G",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " is "
        },
        {
          "text": "−1 −2 0",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " on both rows, and so is every other change. The "
        },
        {
          "text": "G",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " → "
        },
        {
          "text": "B",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " lift belongs to the set, so it applies to both chords in a change equally and cancels when you subtract one grip from the next — which is why the B string never forces you to relearn the movement. It holds as long as the run stays inside one octave copy of the shapes."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "q_triads-ch5-checkpoint.the-slash-that-isnt",
      "kind": "choice",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "text": "A chart prints "
        },
        {
          "text": "C/D",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". What is it asking for?"
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A "
            },
            {
              "text": "C",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " major triad with a "
            },
            {
              "text": "D",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " underneath it. "
            },
            {
              "text": "D",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " isn't one of that chord's three notes, so this isn't an inversion at all — just a bass note the arranger wants."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "C",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " major in second inversion."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Two chords in the bar, "
            },
            {
              "text": "C",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " then "
            },
            {
              "text": "D",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A misprint — the note after a slash is always one of the chord's own notes."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "answerId": "a",
      "explanation": [
        {
          "text": "Everything left of the slash is the chord; everything right of it is the single lowest note, and nothing more. Sometimes that note is already in the chord — "
        },
        {
          "text": "C/E",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " is first inversion, "
        },
        {
          "text": "C/G",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " is second — and sometimes, as here, it isn't. "
        },
        {
          "text": "C/G",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " would have been the second inversion — the reading this one is most often confused with."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "q_triads-ch5-checkpoint.hear-the-inversion",
      "kind": "listen",
      "audio": {
        "kind": "notes",
        "notes": ["G3", "C4", "E4"],
        "mode": "chord"
      },
      "prompt": [
        {
          "text": "A C major triad on strings 1-2-3, with nothing sounding underneath it. Which inversion is it?"
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Root position."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "First inversion."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Second inversion."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "There is no way to tell — the three inversions of a chord all sound the same."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "answerId": "c",
      "explanation": [
        {
          "text": "The lowest note is "
        },
        {
          "text": "G3",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " — the fifth — so the fifth is in the bass and this is second inversion, "
        },
        {
          "text": "x x x 0 1 0",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". The idea that the three inversions all sound alike is worth ruling out for good: inversion is audible to trained and untrained listeners alike, and it ranks in one direction every time, with tension rising and consonance falling from root position through first to second."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "q_triads-ch5-checkpoint.over-a-bass-and-in-time",
      "kind": "multi-select",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "text": "Which of these are true about playing a three-string triad in a band? Select every one that applies."
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "With a bass player in the room, a chart's slash is addressed to them — the chord above it is your job."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Put a "
            },
            {
              "text": "C",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " underneath "
            },
            {
              "text": "x x x 0 1 0",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " and the room hears C major in root position, even though the lowest note of your own grip is the fifth."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Working the same three strings for a whole passage, rather than roving, is what makes the muting reliable."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A comping part should land a chord on every beat; the gaps between chords are dead space."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "e",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Moving a progression down to a lower string set changes which chords you are playing."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "answerIds": ["a", "b", "c"],
      "explanation": [
        {
          "text": "Inversion is decided by the lowest sounding note, so once someone else is playing lower than you, the inversion is theirs and what is left to you is register and how little your hand moves. The muting point is the practical reason three-string triads became a rhythm-guitar habit at all. A chord on every beat has it backwards — a comping part is mostly silence with chords placed into it. And moving down a string set confuses register with harmony: a lower set is the same chords, further down."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
